damaged_code:
```rust
// Write to the source of truth, then read the same key from an
// asynchronously replicated follower / migration target.
async fn save_and_get_user(db: &PrimaryDB, replica: &ReplicaDB, user_id: u64, email: String) -> Result<User, Error> {
    db.write(user_id, &email).await?;
    // BUG: replication lag - the replica may not have applied the write yet,
    // so the caller sees the old email (or no user at all).
    let user = replica.read_user(user_id).await?;
    Ok(user)
}
```

counterexample:
Valid if the application explicitly tolerates stale results and does not use the retrieved value for decisions that require the new state (mark as `Context-dependent`). Routing the read to the primary, or gating the replica read until it has applied the write's version, is also valid:
```rust
async fn update_profile_safe(client: &DbClient, profile: Profile) {
    client.write_primary(&profile).await.unwrap();

    // Force reads of the user's own profile to be served from the primary node,
    // routing replica queries only for other users' profiles.
    let fetched = client.read_primary(profile.user_id).await.unwrap();
}
```

fix:
Route reads of data the session has just written back to the source of truth, or track the write's replication position / timestamp / version vector and block the replica read until the target has applied at least that position [117, 130, 192, 206, 659].
```rust
async fn save_and_get_user_fixed(db: &PrimaryDB, _replica: &ReplicaDB, user_id: u64, email: String) -> Result<User, Error> {
    db.write(user_id, &email).await?;
    let user = db.read_user(user_id).await?; // Read from source of truth
    Ok(user)
}
```
